var tafsirData_59 = {
  "1": "\"Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth glorifies Allah. For He is the Almighty, All-Wise.\" opens Surah al-Hashr exactly as Surah al-Hadid began, establishing continuity. The words repeat that whatever is in heavens and earth already declares Allah free from imperfection, reminding the listener that revelation and law that follow are from the same Glorifier who is Almighty and All-Wise, the manager of heavens, earth, drop, seed, water, and fire.\n\nRepeating \"Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth\" after a chapter that detailed private whispers, alliances, and the wall between light and darkness shows that even conspiratorial hearts operate inside a creation that praises its Lord. Secret talks do not exit His kingdom, just as strongholds do not put anyone out of His reach.\n\n\"For He is the Almighty, All-Wise\" again pairs irresistible might with perfect wisdom. That pairing prepares for the narrative of Banu an-Nadir, whose strongholds seemed mighty yet were overcome by wise decree. Almighty explains that expulsion could occur despite their defenses; All-Wise explains that it occurred with lessons, with mercy for believers, and with distribution of gains that prevents wealth merely circulating among the rich.\n\nThe opening is not decorative. It frames the historical lesson that follows as an act of the Praised who deserves glorification, not a tribal anecdote. Taking a lesson, as the coming verse commands, means seeing beyond politics to the Almighty who humbles defiance and the All-Wise who legislates after it.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to hear \"glorifies\" as merely poetic. The Quran treats it as real worship by creation in ways we do not fully comprehend, inviting humans not to be the only silent party. Remembering universal glorification before recounting expulsion encourages humility when recounting history. It is Allah who removes, who casts horror, and who judges, while human actors are witnesses and agents within His allowing. That awareness keeps the coming lessons from hardening into triumphalism and keeps them as glorification that softens hearts after long time and revives them like earth after rain.",
  "2": "\"He is the One Who expelled the disbelievers of the People of the Book from their homes for their first banishment ever. You never thought they would go. And they thought their strongholds would put them out of Allah's reach. But the decree of Allah came upon them from where they never expected. And He cast horror into their hearts so they destroyed their houses with their own hands and the hands of the believers. So take a lesson from this, O people of insight!\" recounts the expulsion of Banu an-Nadir, a Jewish tribe in Medina that had a covenant with the Muslims, then violated it by plotting against the Prophet. The verse compresses political, military, and spiritual lessons into one narrative sentence.\n\n\"He is the One Who expelled\" attributes the removal to Allah, not merely to the believers' tactics. That framing matches the decree that He and His messengers will prevail and that Satan's party is bound to lose. \"The disbelievers of the People of the Book\" identifies them as a scriptural community that denied despite knowledge, aligning with earlier critique of those whose hearts hardened over long time.\n\n\"You never thought they would go\" names the believers' own surprise. The tribe's palms, forts, and alliances looked impregnable, and many companions assumed a prolonged stalemate. \"And they thought their strongholds would put them out of Allah's reach\" captures their confidence in architecture and walls, the same \"fortified strongholds or from behind walls\" later described as the only places hypocrites and their allies dare fight from. The Quran exposes that reliance on walls is psychological as much as military.\n\n\"But the decree of Allah came upon them from where they never expected\" explains reversal. It came through internal horror rather than only external siege. \"He cast horror into their hearts\" describes divinely placed dread that unraveled resolve. That horror led them to \"destroy their houses with their own hands,\" stripping wood and doors to prevent benefit to the Muslims or to signal departure, while \"the hands of the believers\" completed the removal. The self-destruction shows how defiance can turn a people into agents of their own loss.\n\nThe closing command, \"So take a lesson from this, O people of insight,\" universalizes the event. Insight, or \"ulu al-absar,\" means those who look beyond surface causes to see divine patterns. The lesson is multiple. Trust not in strongholds, honor covenants, expect divine decree from unexpected quarter, and receive community gains with humility and law that follows, so that wealth does not merely circulate among the rich.",
  "3": "\"Had Allah not decreed exile for them, He would have certainly punished them in this world. And in the Hereafter they will suffer the punishment of the Fire.\" explains that expulsion was itself a减轻ed outcome. After describing the surprising expulsion of the disbelievers among the People of the Book from homes they thought impregnable, the verse clarifies that exile was decreed instead of a harsher immediate punishment that their treachery had earned.\n\n\"Had Allah not decreed exile\" shows divine measure. Exile, or \"jala',\" leaving homes and resettling elsewhere, is painful and disruptive, yet it preserves life and possibility of reflection. The Quran says this milder decree was written; otherwise, a more severe worldly punishment such as execution or total ruin, which befell some earlier peoples who violently betrayed covenants, would have occurred. The believers who thought the strongholds would never fall are reminded that the actual fall was moderated by mercy and wisdom, not merely by strategy.\n\n\"And in the Hereafter they will suffer the punishment of the Fire\" completes the accounting. Being spared a maximal worldly punishment does not erase accountability before the appointed gathering where Allah keeps account while they forget. Exile ends their local power and gives respite to reconsider, but persistent denial without repentance leaves the final Fire as recompense.\n\nThis pairing reflects the pattern seen with the harvest and water that could be withheld, the soul that could be held at the throat, and the clear revelations that were sent to bring from darkness to light. Warnings and punishments in this life are calibrated to awaken, not merely to destroy, giving time while the soul has not yet reached the throat.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read exile as mere political displacement without moral meaning or to use the verse to justify harshness toward displaced peoples in general. The Quran isolates this case as covenant betrayal by a specific tribe allied with hypocrites, not a blanket verdict on every People of the Book, and it repeatedly praises faithful among them.\n\nFor practical reflection, the verse invites taking lessons rather than gloating, as the previous verse commanded. It also encourages gratitude when a lesser corrective arrives instead of a greater one, prompting prompt repentance before the Fire that is described as truly severe and humiliating, so that temporary displacement in this world does not become permanent loss in the next.",
  "4": "\"This is because they defied Allah and His Messenger. And whoever defies Allah, then Allah is truly severe in punishment.\" gives the cause behind exile and the greater punishment that would have otherwise come. The pronoun \"This\" points back to expulsion, horror, and self-destruction of houses just described.\n\n\"Because they defied Allah and His Messenger\" translates \"shaqqu Allah wa Rasulahu,\" literally they split away, took the opposite side, and cut themselves off from the direction of revelation and balance. Their defiance was not private doubt, but covenant betrayal, plotting against the Prophet in Medina after having agreed to peaceful terms. Standing on the opposing border led them, as earlier verses warned, to be among the most debased, just like those before them.\n\nThe general principle, \"And whoever defies Allah, then Allah is truly severe in punishment,\" extends the lesson beyond this tribe. It universalizes the insight commanded as \"take a lesson, O people of insight.\" Severity here does not indicate hasty cruelty, but firm, inescapable justice from the Almighty, All-Wise who also sent Scripture and balance so that people may administer justice and who still offers double mercy, light, and forgiveness to those who turn.\n\nClassical tafsir notes that divine severity is often mentioned alongside spacious mercy to prevent both despair and complacency. The same chapter will later say Allah is the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful while also Severe in punishment, preserving accurate awe without closing the door to return.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to apply severe punishment to every mistake. The Quran reserves this intensity for defiance, persistence after clear proofs, mockery of revelation, and betrayal that harms community, especially when coupled with allying against believers and hindering from Allah's way.\n\nFor present conduct, remembering severity restrains the arrogance that strongholds, wealth, or alliances make one unreachable, and it underscores the need to keep private talk for goodness, to honor covenants, and to glorify the Lord of heavens and earth who sees whatever enters the earth and ascends, trusting that severe justice will not touch those who sincerely fear Allah, believe in His Messenger, and compete for forgiveness.",
  "5": "\"Whatever palm trees you believers cut down or left standing intact, it was all by Allah's Will, so that He might disgrace the rebellious.\" addresses a specific criticism during the siege of Banu an-Nadir, concerning palm trees cut to pressure the besieged. After stating cause and severity for defiance, the verse legitimizes and interprets that tactical action.\n\n\"Whatever palm trees you believers cut down or left standing\" acknowledges that some palms were felled while others were preserved. Both choices were made under the Messenger's direction within the norms of conflict where destroying productive assets was otherwise discouraged. The Quran does not hide the act, but places it within divine permission.\n\n\"It was all by Allah's Will\" translates \"bi-idhn Allah,\" by Allah's permission and knowledge, not by arbitrary plunder. The cutting was not a private initiative for personal gain, but a measured step to end betrayal quickly and to demonstrate that material resources considered protection could not save a covenant betrayal when the decree came from unexpected quarter. Leaving some standing showed restraint and future provision for the community.\n\n\"So that He might disgrace the rebellious\" gives the purpose. \"Disgrace\" translates \"yukhzi al-fasiqin,\" to humiliate and expose those who persistently rebel and break trusts. Seeing prized palms cut while strongholds crumbled signaled that wealth and agriculture, when relied upon as shields against divine justice, become instruments of exposure. The same palms that fed luxury that spoiled them became a sign of vulnerability.\n\nClassical jurists derive nuanced law from this verse. The general principle in Islam is to protect trees and productive assets in conflict, as exemplified by Abu Bakr's instructions, with exceptional, limited cutting permitted only under legitimate authority for clear, just purpose to end aggression, not for wanton waste.\n\nA modern misunderstanding would be to use this verse to justify environmental destruction. The verse limits permission to what Allah willed at that specific siege to disgrace rebellion, and it pairs cutting with leaving intact, indicating selectivity.\n\nPractically, the lesson is to trust that lawful authority, guided by balance and Scripture, may take measured steps to protect community, but that the norm remains preservation, gratitude for palms and water previously celebrated as provision, and disgust for rebellion that forces even disliked measures.",
  "6": "\"As for the gains Allah has turned over to His Messenger from them—you did not even spur on any horse or camel for such gains. But Allah gives authority to His messengers over whoever He wills. For Allah is Most Capable of everything.\" explains the nature of the gains, or \"fay',\" that came to the believers after Banu an-Nadir's expulsion without a major battle.\n\n\"You did not even spur on any horse or camel for such gains\" tells the companions that this particular wealth was not obtained through cavalry charge or exhausting campaign. The tribe surrendered and left after the siege and horror cast into hearts, so there was no spoils of battle distributed by the usual shares that depend on fighting effort. This clarification prevents dispute over division, since many had anticipated distribution by customary war rules.\n\n\"But Allah gives authority to His messengers over whoever He wills\" places the matter in divine sovereignty. The Messenger distributes these gains under divine instruction for the common good, rather than participants dividing them by personal effort. The authority is not for private enrichment, as his own lifestyle of simplicity showed, but for administering justice with the balance sent with Scripture.\n\n\"For Allah is Most Capable of everything\" reinforces that outcomes like expulsion without horse or camel, horror cast into fortified hearts, and provision without sowing are easy for Allah, as was said about writing every calamity and blessing before creation.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think gains are merely tribute to a ruler. The next verses detail the recipients: Allah and His Messenger, close relatives, orphans, poor, and needy travelers, ensuring wealth does not merely circulate among the rich.\n\nFor today, this verse teaches that some provisions arrive without the usual striving, and their proper handling is to trust prophetic guidance about distribution, to avoid quarreling over windfalls, and to see such gains as community trusts to strengthen the vulnerable, support those who left homes seeking bounty and pleasure while standing for Allah and His Messenger, and maintain the community's balance where iron may protect but does not replace mercy.",
  "7": "\"As for gains granted by Allah to His Messenger from the people of other lands, they are for Allah and the Messenger, his close relatives, orphans, the poor, and needy travellers so that wealth may not merely circulate among your rich. Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it. And whatever he forbids you from, leave it. And fear Allah. Surely Allah is severe in punishment.\" lays down the permanent distribution principle for \"fay'\" after the Banu an-Nadir case. The verse moves from a specific expulsion to a general rule.\n\nThe recipients are listed deliberately. \"For Allah and the Messenger\" indicates the share is managed under divine direction, not for personal accumulation, as the Prophet lived austerely. \"His close relatives\" refers to Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib, who were restricted from ordinary alms and thus supported here to free them from needing to ask the community's obligatory charity. \"Orphans, the poor, and needy travellers\" are three classic vulnerable categories whose needs persist regardless of tribe or wealth, aligning with earlier calls to lend a good loan and to revive hardened hearts through generosity.\n\nThe reason, \"so that wealth may not merely circulate among your rich,\" states an economic ethic. Gains should not concentrate and cycle only among the wealthy, widening gaps and hardening hearts after long time. Distribution aims to re-circulate provision toward those in need and for public good, reflecting the balance sent so that people may administer justice and countering the boasting and competition in wealth condemned earlier.\n\nThe great command, \"Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it. And whatever he forbids you from, leave it,\" extends beyond spoils to all prophetic guidance. It establishes obedience to the Messenger as obedience to Allah in legislation, worship, and social ordering, a standard that earlier divided the faithful who make room and give room from the rebellious who defy.\n\n\"And fear Allah. Surely Allah is severe in punishment\" warns against taking what is forbidden or hoarding what should be shared.\n\nA modern application is to design community finance to prevent wealth merely circulating among the rich, through fair wages, support for emigrants and travelers, care for orphans, and respect for trusted leadership. The verse also counsels individuals to accept prophetic instruction even when desire prefers otherwise, trusting the Severe in punishment who is also the Lord of infinite bounty and who has promised multiplied, honourable reward for the generous.",
  "8": "\"Some of the gains will be for poor emigrants who were driven out of their homes and wealth, seeking Allah's bounty and pleasure, and standing up for Allah and His Messenger. They are the ones true in faith.\" identifies the first blessed recipient group named in the distribution of gains, the poor emigrants, or \"muhajirun,\" who left Mecca for Medina.\n\nThe description \"driven out of their homes and wealth\" captures coercion, not voluntary migration for profit. They were expelled simply for belief in Allah, as earlier verses noted about exiles driven out for saying our Lord is Allah. Their homes, shops, and fields were left behind, making them truly poor upon arrival despite earlier standing.\n\n\"Seeking Allah's bounty and pleasure, and standing up for Allah and His Messenger\" gives their motive. Bounty, or \"fadl,\" includes provision in this life and in the next, while pleasure, or \"ridwan,\" points to divine acceptance, the same mutual pleasure described for the party of Allah who are admitted to gardens to stay forever. Their seeking was coupled with standing up, or \"yansuruna,\" active support for the message that brings from darkness to light, not merely private piety. They donated and fought before the victory when rank was greater, lending good loans despite need.\n\n\"They are the ones true in faith\" translates \"as-sadiqun,\" the truly truthful. Earlier the Quran called those who believe in Allah and His messengers the people of truth, and here the emigrants are singled as exemplars, because their migration proved that their words matched deeds. They left kin, comfort, and status for belief, bearing witness with action.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to view gains as repayment for ethnic belonging. They are for truthful sacrifice and purpose, not lineage alone. The next verse will praise the Ansar who shared generously despite need, showing that both giving emigrants and generous hosts are honored.\n\nFor today, honoring the emigrants means caring for those driven from homes and wealth in contemporary exiles, ensuring gains and aid do not merely circulate among the rich but reach the displaced who seek bounty and pleasure through dignified work and standing for justice, recognizing them as potentially the most truthful among the community, whose needs should be met with the honourable, multiplied reward the Gracious promised.",
  "9": "\"As for those who had settled in the city and embraced the faith before the arrival of the emigrants, they love whoever immigrates to them, never having a desire in their hearts for whatever of the gains is given to the emigrants. They give the emigrants preference over themselves even though they may be in need. And whoever is saved from the selfishness of their own souls, it is they who are truly successful.\" celebrates the Ansar, the helpers of Medina who had embraced faith before the emigrants arrived and made room in households, hearts, and gatherings.\n\n\"They love whoever immigrates to them\" describes affection without resentment, even as newcomers shared limited housing, water, and palms. Their love mirrors the compassion instilled in followers of Jesus, now shown among followers of Muhammad. It also embodies making room when told to make room, for which Allah promises to make room in grace.\n\n\"Never having a desire in their hearts for whatever of the gains is given to the emigrants\" indicates purity from envy or hidden resentment over distribution. They did not grumble that fay' or support favored newcomers. This inward cleanliness matches the revived heart humble at remembrance, not the hardened heart after long time.\n\n\"They give the emigrants preference over themselves even though they may be in need\" captures \"yu'thirun,\" preferring others despite \"khasasah,\" neediness or scarcity. Authentic reports illustrate this, including offering to share dates, homes, and even proposing to divide produce, while the emigrants responded with dignity, preferring work. The Quran does not command self-harm, but praises this level of generosity as exemplary, while law encourages balance so no one is left destitute.\n\n\"And whoever is saved from the selfishness of their own souls, it is they who are truly successful\" universalizes the lesson. \"Shuhh,\" selfishness or niggardliness of the soul, is the inner stinginess that earlier verses linked to promoting stinginess and boasting. Being saved from it is true success, or \"falah,\" the same great triumph described for the Gardens.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think preferring others means neglecting one's own family's rights. The Quran praises their choice while still requiring just provision for dependents. The intended takeaway is to cultivate generosity that stretches beyond comfort, trusting that the Self-Sufficient will not forsake the preferrer and that Allah is All-Aware of such beautiful doing.",
  "10": "\"And those who come after them will pray, \\\"Our Lord! Forgive us and our fellow believers who preceded us in faith, and do not allow bitterness into our hearts towards those who believe. Our Lord! Indeed, You are Ever Gracious, Most Merciful.\\\"\" gives the supplication of later generations after the emigrants and the Ansar. The verse forms a bridge from praised history to ongoing community, teaching the attitude that keeps hearts from hardening over long time.\n\nThe supplicants are \"those who come after them,\" meaning every believer in later generations up to the Hour, who did not witness migration or early victories but still benefits from gains, knowledge, and example. Their first request, \"Forgive us and our fellow believers who preceded us in faith,\" shows humility and solidarity. Instead of judging predecessors or claiming superiority, they seek forgiveness for themselves and for those who went before, including the emigrants who were expelled and the helpers who preferred others despite need. This mirrors the Quranic counsel to revere earlier prophets Noah and Abraham without claiming ancestry alone suffices.\n\nThe second request, \"and do not allow bitterness into our hearts towards those who believe,\" asks for inner purity against envy, rancor, and factional hatred, the very diseases that led some to secret talks of sin and to allying with displeased peoples, and that caused earlier hearts to harden after long time. Bitterness, or \"ghill,\" is the residue of envy and grudge that sours gatherings and prevents making room sincerely. Asking Allah to prevent it acknowledges that hearts are in His hands, not merely the product of willpower.\n\nThe closing address, \"Our Lord! Indeed, You are Ever Gracious, Most Merciful,\" returns to two names that embody compassion and mercy, the same names behind universal glorification and measured writing of calamity and blessing. Graciousness promises pardon for shortcomings that remain despite striving; mercy promises continued joining to the faithful community.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to limit this prayer to ritual utterance. The verse shapes community culture. Speaking well of predecessors, refraining from slander about respected companions and teachers, and actively seeking to love whoever believes, regardless of tribe or timing, are lived forms of this prayer. The good loan, fair distribution of gains, and humble remembrance all flow more easily from a heart without bitterness that has learned to pray for those before and alongside it.",
  "11": "\"Have you O Prophet not seen the hypocrites who say to their fellow disbelievers from the People of the Book, \\\"If you are expelled, we will certainly leave with you, and We will never obey anyone against you. And if you are fought against, we will surely help you.\\\"? But Allah bears witness that they are truly liars.\" exposes the false alliance behind the Banu an-Nadir resistance. After praising the emigrants, helpers, and later supplicants, the verse contrasts them with hypocrites whose promises proved empty.\n\n\"Have you O Prophet not seen the hypocrites who say to their fellow disbelievers from the People of the Book\" addresses the Messenger about a specific episode. Hypocrites in Medina led by Abdullah ibn Ubayy sent messages to Banu an-Nadir urging them to hold firm, promising joint exodus and military support, and claiming they would disregard any pressure from Muslims. Fellow disbelievers indicates partnership in denial despite differing labels.\n\nThe quoted promise is absolute: \"we will certainly leave with you\" and \"we will never obey anyone against you\" and \"we will surely help you if you are fought.\" The language uses emphatic oaths, the same false shields earlier condemned as lies knowingly sworn. It presents loyalty as unconditional, feeding the hosts' illusion that strongholds would put them out of Allah's reach.\n\nThe divine response, \"But Allah bears witness that they are truly liars,\" nullifies the claim at its root. Allah's witness outweighs any oath they swore. He is All-Seeing of what you do, Knower of what is hidden in the heart, and Witness over all things, whose account remains while they forget. History confirmed the witness. When expulsion came, hypocrites remained behind, taking no step to leave.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to broaden this to justify suspicion of every interfaith contact. The lie lies in sworn, collusive promises to defy and hinder, not in ordinary neighborly dealings. Fair dealing with those who do not fight or expel believers remains commanded.\n\nFor reflection, the verse warns against making promises for display that one does not intend to keep, especially promises that encourage others to resist just resolution. True standing for Allah without seeing Him is proven by consistency at cost, not by emphatic words before consequence arrives.",
  "12": "\"Indeed, if they are expelled, the hypocrites will never leave with them. And if they are fought against, the hypocrites will never help them. And even if the hypocrites did so, they would certainly flee, then the disbelievers would be left with no help.\" fulfills the divine witness that the previous verse declared. After quoting the hypocrites' emphatic promises to Banu an-Nadir, the Quran now announces what will actually happen, in three stages of exposure.\n\n\"If they are expelled, the hypocrites will never leave with them\" was fulfilled when the tribe departed and hypocrites stayed in Medina, comfortable rather than covenant-bound. Their promise of shared exile proved empty, showing that alliance rested on convenient speech, not on shared conviction. \"And if they are fought against, the hypocrites will never help them\" points to anticipated battle that did not fully materialize here but whose principle held in later confrontations. The helpers who were supposed to relieve the siege never arrived.\n\nThe third forecast adds a hypothetical that removes even imagined courage: \"And even if the hypocrites did so, they would certainly flee, then the disbelievers would be left with no help.\" Even if they had mustered a show of support, their hearts had already been diagnosed as fearing people more than Allah, with hearts divided and no real understanding. Flight would expose disunity, as described where even united they dare not fight except from fortified strongholds or behind walls.\n\nThis triple certainty mirrors the earlier exposition that secret talks grieve believers but cannot harm except by Allah's will. The believer's trust should therefore be in Allah, the All-Powerful, Almighty whose decree is that He and His messengers will prevail, not in the loud promises of an unreliable party whose oaths are shields.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to gloat over ancient failures. The Quran records the failure to teach insight, not to breed arrogance. The lesson is about reliability of commitments. Do not tie community security to those whose loyalty shifts with convenience or who swear to lies knowingly. For personal character, the verse invites self-examination. Are my promises to stand for justice and to support the faithful conditional on ease, or will they hold when leaving comfort is required? Learning to keep contracts, as earlier praised, and to mean private and public words alike is how a believer avoids becoming the person this verse describes.",
  "13": "\"Indeed, there is more fear in their hearts for you believers than for Allah. That is because they are a people who do not comprehend.\" diagnoses the inner state of the combined hypocrites and their disbelieving allies discussed in the previous verses. Their outward alliance looks united, but inwardly their fear is misdirected.\n\n\"More fear in their hearts for you believers than for Allah\" describes a group that fears human censure, loss of status, or a siege's pressure more than they fear the Almighty, All-Wise who expelled them from where they never expected, who keeps account while they forget, and whose punishment is severe. Earlier they asked, \"Why does Allah not punish us for what we say?\" mistaking delay for absence, and now they shrink before people while forgetting the One who decrees both victory and exile.\n\nThis misplaced fear leads to cowardice before believers and recklessness before Allah. They may perform brave speeches about never obeying anyone against their allies, yet when standing before the community of faith that prays, pays alms, and trusts in Allah, they tremble, withhold help, and flee, as just foretold. Their alliance was never rooted in conviction, so admiration of people cannot sustain it under pressure.\n\n\"That is because they are a people who do not comprehend\" gives cause. \"Do not comprehend\" translates \"la yafqahuna,\" lacking deep, practical understanding that connects knowledge to action. They do not comprehend that the kingdom belongs to Allah, that all matters are returned to Him, that He is with you wherever you are, and that He is the First and Last. If they did, they would fear the eternal consequence of defiance more than the temporary discomfort of honoring a covenant or admitting error.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this verse praises believers for being feared. The commendation is not that believers should cultivate intimidation, but that false parties naturally fear the cohesion of the faithful, honest community, precisely because that cohesion exposes their own disunity.\n\nFor believers, the lesson inverts the diagnosis. Cultivate greater fear of Allah than of people by remembering the appointed Day, by fearing to grieve believers with sinful secret talks, and by honoring trust. That proper fear steadies courage, allowing one to keep promises, to give the good loan, and to stand for balance and iron with calm, because the One most to be feared is also the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful who multiplies reward for the faithful.",
  "14": "\"Even united, they would not dare fight against you except from within fortified strongholds or from behind walls. Their malice for each other is intense: you think they are united, yet their hearts are divided. That is because they are a people with no real understanding.\" completes the portrait of the hypocrite-disbeliever alliance by describing their battlefield behavior and inner disunity. The verse follows the diagnosis that they fear people more than Allah due to lack of comprehension, now showing how that fear and lack manifest in action.\n\n\"Even united, they would not dare fight against you except from within fortified strongholds or from behind walls\" captures cowardice conditioned on cover. \"Even united\" indicates that numbers do not cure fear when hearts lack trust in Allah. \"Fortified strongholds\" and \"walls\" recall both Banu an-Nadir's actual forts and the general metaphor for hiding behind barriers, whether literal fortifications or modern shields like anonymity, propaganda, or false oaths used as protection. They advance only when they believe risk is minimal and retreat is assured.\n\n\"Their malice for each other is intense: you think they are united, yet their hearts are divided\" moves from external posture to internal reality. Observers might see one camp and assume solidarity, but the Quran reveals that mutual malice is strong and hearts are scattered. The hypocrites privately despise the very allies they flattered, and the disbelievers distrust the hypocrites' empty promises, just as earlier their oaths were described as shields that do not cohere into true party loyalty. This division is the opposite of believers who are asked to make room in gatherings and whose hearts are asked not to harbor bitterness toward fellow believers.\n\n\"That is because they are a people with no real understanding\" repeats the diagnosis with \"la ya'qiluna,\" lacking reasoned, applied intelligence. Comprehension may grasp information; understanding applies it to choose the party of Allah over the party of Satan.\n\nA modern lesson is that appearances of unity in deceptive alliances should not intimidate the faithful into abandoning goodness and righteousness in private talk or stinginess in public giving. Cohesion of the faithful, built on forgiveness sought for predecessors, sharing beyond need, and light on the Day, outlasts cover-seeking coalitions whose walls cannot keep out the decree that comes from where they never expected.",
  "15": "\"They are both just like those who recently went down before them: they tasted the evil consequences of their doings. And they will suffer a painful punishment.\" links the hypocrite-disbeliever coalition facing expulsion to a recent historical precedent and to a future certainty. The pronoun \"They are both\" refers to the hypocrites and their fellow disbelievers together, their fates joined because their deception was joined.\n\n\"Just like those who recently went down before them\" points to a proximate example, understood by most commentators as Banu Qaynuqa', another Jewish tribe in Medina expelled after betrayal shortly before Banu an-Nadir, or more generally polytheists who suffered at Badr soon before these verses. Recency matters. The memory was fresh, so the lesson should have been vivid and deterrent. They had witnessed tangible failure yet learned nothing, as hearts hardened after long time tend to do.\n\n\"They tasted the evil consequences of their doings\" translates \"wabala amrihim,\" the heavy, bitter outcome of their chosen affair. Tasting indicates direct, personal experience, not hearsay. Their plotting, secret talks, false shields, and reliance on strongholds became a bitter flavor in the mouth, the opposite of the good and pure speech promised for those in Gardens.\n\n\"And they will suffer a painful punishment\" adds that the tasting in this world does not exhaust recompense. Expulsion, loss of homes they destroyed with their own hands, and social disgrace were tastes. The painful punishment in the next world, where severe, humiliating, Fire-bound consequences await those who defy Allah and His Messenger and swear to lies, remains as the extended recompense.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as delight in others' pain. The Quran uses recent history as mercy, inviting insight before repetition. The command \"take a lesson, O people of insight\" applies again here, urging believers and opponents alike to see that defiance ends in tasting evil outcomes and should be abandoned while taste can still be followed by return.\n\nPractically, the verse counsels humility about near misses. If a group has seen another alliance fail yet continues the same tactics of secret grieving talks and allying with displeASURE, they are walking toward tasting the same bitterness. Turning to humility at remembrance, to charity, and to standing for justice is the way to replace bitter tasting with serenity and fragrance promised to those brought near.",
  "16": "\"They are like Satan when he lures someone to disbelieve. Then after they have done so, he will say on Judgment Day, \\\"I have absolutely nothing to do with you. I truly fear Allah—the Lord of all worlds.\\\"\" deepens the comparison for the hypocrite-disbeliever alliance by likening it to Satan's classic deception. The verse follows the note that they tasted evil consequences, now showing the pattern behind that tasting.\n\n\"They are like Satan when he lures someone to disbelieve\" captures the instigator who promises help and then vanishes. Satan's lure offers boldness, promising that rebellion will bring honor, safety, or profit, just as hypocrites told Banu an-Nadir to stay firm, promising exodus and support. The victim, believing the hype, commits, and then the lure is withdrawn.\n\n\"Then after they have done so, he will say on Judgment Day\" sets the scene of abandonment at the very moment blame matters. Satan's declaration, \"I have absolutely nothing to do with you. I truly fear Allah—the Lord of all worlds,\" is quoted as his disowning. Classical tafsir notes the irony. The one who encouraged defiance now professes fear of Allah and briskly cuts ties, leaving his follower to face the painful punishment alone. This mirrors the hypocrites' own behavior. They promised \"we will never obey anyone against you\" and then stayed behind, and even if they had come, they would have fled, leaving their allies without help.\n\nThis double layer suggests the hypocrites themselves resemble Satan in both deceit and desertion, and the whole coalition resembles the human who followed Satan and is then disowned by him. The chain of imitation ends in isolation.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to treat Satan's claim to fear Allah as sincere piety. The Quran presents it as a self-serving dissociation, true in utterance but false in loyalty, spoken to distance himself rather than to repent genuinely. Fear without return is not the saving fear that leads to belief and the good loan.\n\nFor listeners, the verse teaches to judge promises by their source and by readiness to stand when hardship arrives, not by emphatic oaths before it. Seek companions who instill compassion and mercy, not those who lure toward sin and then disappear when the soul reaches the throat or when matters are returned to Allah.",
  "17": "\"So they will both end up in the Fire, staying there forever. That is the reward of the wrongdoers.\" gives the joint outcome of the deceiver and the deceived after Satan's disavowal. The pronoun \"they will both\" indicates Satan's follower and the one who lured like Satan, understood here as the hypocrites and their fellow disbelievers who allied against Allah and His Messenger, now bound together in recompense just as they were bound in plotting.\n\n\"End up in the Fire, staying there forever\" describes permanence for those who persist in defiance without repentance until death. Earlier the Quran said exile was decreed instead of a harsher worldly punishment, and that a severe, humiliating punishment awaited, and that neither wealth nor children would help. Now that punishment is named and its duration clarified as abiding, not passing, for the persistent wrongdoer. \"Forever\" reflects the settled choice to remain with the party of Satan, who are bound to lose, rather than moving to the party of Allah, who are bound to succeed.\n\n\"That is the reward of the wrongdoers\" labels the outcome as just recompense, not arbitrary cruelty. \"Wrongdoers\" translates \"zalimin,\" those who wrong by putting things in the wrong place, placing oaths where truth should be, placing alliance with displeased people where loyalty to the Messenger should be, and placing reliance on walls where reliance on Allah should be. The Fire is described as the appropriate equivalence for that systematic misplacement.\n\nThe verse closes the narrative arc that began with Banu an-Nadir's strongholds and the hypocrites' promises. Horror, self-destruction of houses, exposure as liars, fear of people over fear of Allah, fighting only from walls, divided hearts, tasting evil consequences, and satanic luring all culminate in this end, showing coherence between earthly pattern and final recompense, between secret talk that grieved believers and Fire that grieves its residents permanently.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think this forecloses mercy for the living. As long as death has not arrived, the call to be humbled at remembrance, to revive dead hearts, to lend the good loan, and to pray without bitterness remains open. The verse invites those still able to choose to leave the coalition of wrongdoing, to join the praised emigrants and helpers in practical generosity, and to avoid the reward of wrongdoers by doing what is placed rightly.",
  "18": "\"O believers! Be mindful of Allah and let every soul look to what deeds it has sent forth for tomorrow. And fear Allah, for certainly Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\" shifts from the joint doom of the wrongdoing alliance to a direct call for individual accountability. The address \"O believers\" shows that even after praising emigrants, helpers, and later supplicants, every soul still needs self-examination.\n\n\"Be mindful of Allah\" translates \"ittaqu Allah,\" maintaining reverent awareness that Allah hears the woman's plea, knows whatever is in heavens and earth, is with you wherever you are, and keeps account while people forget. Mindfulness is the positive counterpart to Satan causing forgetting of remembrance. It restores the balance sent so people may administer justice and keeps private talk oriented to goodness and righteousness.\n\n\"Let every soul look to what deeds it has sent forth for tomorrow\" uses a striking metaphor. \"Tomorrow\" is the next life, whose Day is the appointed Day, the tomorrow to today's today. Deeds are portrayed as ahead, sent forth like advance provision or investment, like wealth that does not merely circulate among the rich but is forwarded to the eternal treasury. This echoes the good loan that is multiplied and the charity men and women that receives honourable reward. The verse asks each soul to audit that forward shipment. What has my soul already sent? Quality matters more than show. Sincere, hidden charity, truthful oaths, and making room in gatherings are quietly sent ahead where the Record preserves them.\n\n\"And fear Allah, for certainly Allah is All-Aware of what you do\" repeats fear as reverent caution and grounds it in perfect awareness. The All-Aware sees both sent deeds and omitted duties, and will inform all on the Day when all are gathered.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as anxiety-provoking self-obsession. The intended fear is constructive, leading to hope, as the next verse contrasts forgetting Allah with remembering Him, and as earlier promise of double mercy, light, and forgiveness follows fear and belief. Practically, looking to what is sent for tomorrow can be done nightly, reviewing speech, charity, and covenant-keeping, and then choosing one concrete good to send forward next morning, trusting that the reviver of dead earth can revive a dull soul to look ahead wisely.",
  "19": "\"And do not be like those who forgot Allah, so He made them forget themselves. It is they who are truly rebellious.\" warns against the opposite of being mindful and looking to what is sent for tomorrow. After commanding mindfulness and self-audit, the verse describes a group whose forgetting led to self-loss.\n\n\"Those who forgot Allah\" includes neglecting remembrance, ignoring clear proofs, dismissing the balance of justice, and treating the noble Quran lightly after it was sent from the Lord of all worlds. Forgetting here is not simple memory lapse, but willful disregard, as when hypocrites chose to be tempted, awaited demise of believers, doubted, and were deluded by false hopes until death's decree came.\n\n\"So He made them forget themselves\" states the consequence. When Allah is forgotten, concern for one's own soul, benefit, and ultimate home is lost. A person forgets what deeds are being sent for tomorrow, forgets that wealth and children will not help against Allah, and forgets that ransom will not be accepted. Self-forgetting is profound. A person may meticulously manage wealth's circulation among the rich or guard strongholds, yet forget the self that will stand alone when the soul reaches the throat and is nearer to Allah than onlookers can see.\n\n\"It is they who are truly rebellious\" translates \"humu al-fasiqun,\" those who persistently deviate from the path. Rebellion here is not sporadic lapse, but settled departure from the covenant already taken. The label follows the diagnosis rather than preceding it, showing that rebellion is both cause and effect of forgetting.\n\nThis verse mirrors the earlier diagnosis that Satan has taken hold of them, causing them to forget remembrance, and that Satan's party is bound to lose. Forgetting Allah invites satanic takeover; remembrance repels it.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think self-exaltation equals self-care. The verse shows that true self-care requires Allah-remembrance. Ignoring Him while chasing play, amusement, luxury, boasting, and competition in wealth and children appears as self-assertion, but ends as self-forgetting.\n\nFor daily renewal, cultivate deliberate remembrance. Humble the heart when Allah is remembered, revive the heart like revived earth, and check regularly what the soul has sent forward, so that forgetting does not settle and the soul remains present to itself before it is asked about what it did.",
  "20": "\"The residents of the Fire cannot be equal to the residents of Paradise. Only the residents of Paradise will be successful.\" states a foundational inequality after calling believers to be mindful and not to forget Allah. The verse makes explicit what the preceding contrasts implied between Satan's party and Allah's party, between debasement and elevation, between torment and grace, and between strongholds that fail and balance that sustains.\n\n\"The residents of the Fire cannot be equal to the residents of Paradise\" denies equivalence. \"Residents\" again suggests abiding dwelling, not passing visit. Residents of the Fire are those who remained with defiance, hypocrisy, and hindering until death without return, those for whom severe, humiliating punishment was prepared and for whom ransom will not be accepted. Residents of Paradise are the party of Allah, those in whose hearts faith was instilled and who were strengthened with spirit, who made room, gave the good loan, and asked for forgiveness without bitterness. Placing them as equals would betray justice, wisdom, and the writing in the Record where every calamity and blessing was inscribed before creation. The well-preserved Record guards distinction precisely to render it on the Day when all matters are returned.\n\n\"Only the residents of Paradise will be successful\" defines true success, or \"fawz,\" as the attainment of forgiveness and pleasure of Allah and a Garden as vast as heavens and earth, not as worldly play, luxury, or competition won in this life that withers to chaff. The earlier verse \"No calamity... without being written\" was given so people would not grieve excessively over missed worldly wins or boast over gains, because real winning is elsewhere. \"Only\" stresses exclusivity. Those who imagined they succeeded through cunning alliances and shielding oaths will find success did not accompany them beyond the wall, whose one side is grace and the other torment.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to use this verse to excuse neglecting worldly duties, as if work and family are irrelevant because only Paradise matters. The Quran praises iron with benefits, planting, and kind dealings, but subordinates them to ultimate success.\n\nPractically, this verse invites recalibration. Measure decisions by whether they send forward deeds for the tomorrow that determines lasting residence, joining those who remember rather than forget, who prefer others despite need, and who seek a success that the Almighty, All-Wise has prepared and promised to multiply.",
  "21": "\"Had We sent down this Quran upon a mountain, you would have certainly seen it humbled and torn apart in awe of Allah. We set forth such comparisons for people, so perhaps they may reflect.\" gives a powerful image of the Quran's weight immediately after defining ultimate success. The verse moves from future residences to present revelation's majesty.\n\n\"Had We sent down this Quran upon a mountain\" uses a mountain, the archetype of stability and mass, the same kind of mountain described earlier as humbled and torn apart would be at the thought of being entrusted with guidance. In Surah al-Waqi'ah, mountains were crushed to scattered dust at the Inevitable Event. Here a mountain is imagined as recipient of the Quran before that Day, showing that its words carry a gravity that would shake even the unshakeable.\n\n\"You would have certainly seen it humbled and torn apart in awe of Allah\" describes the mountain's response. Humbled, or \"khashi'an,\" is bowed, lowered, and subdued in reverence, not in despair. Torn apart, or \"mutasaddi'an,\" is split, cracked open under the impact of awe. Awe here is \"khashyat Allah,\" reverent fear that recognizes true greatness and responds with softness. The same hardness that afflicted human hearts after long time is contrasted with a mountain that would soften instantly before the same revelation that some humans take lightly or repay with denial.\n\n\"We set forth such comparisons for people, so perhaps they may reflect\" explains method. Quranic parables translate unseen weight into familiar images, like plants that delight then wither to chaff, or transactions like a good loan. Comparison invites reflection, \"yatafakkarun,\" deep thinking that leads to action, not mere appreciation of eloquence.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to read this as praise of dramatic emotional display alone. The mountain's imagined humility is instructive; human reflection should lead to concrete responses already commanded, being humbled at remembrance rather than hardened, reviving the heart like revived earth, and lending the good loan.\n\nFor practice, approaching the Quran as a mountain would, with humility and readiness to be moved, transforms recitation. Instead of rushing, pause, allow awe to soften, and let that softness shape charity, honesty in gatherings, and remembrance that protects from Satan's takeover and prepares for the day when only residents of Paradise are truly successful.",
  "22": "\"He is Allah—there is no god worthy of worship except Him: Knower of the seen and unseen. He is the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful.\" opens the sequence of magnificent divine names that close Surah al-Hashr. After the humbling comparison of the Quran and the mountain, the passage turns to the One who sent the Quran, summarizing lordship, knowledge, and mercy.\n\n\"He is Allah—there is no god worthy of worship except Him\" states pure monotheism, or \"tawhid,\" after a chapter that exposed alliances built on fear of people more than fear of Allah and loyalty to displeased peoples. No stronghold, wealth, child, oath, or party deserves the devotion owed to the Owner of the kingdom.\n\n\"Knower of the seen and unseen\" translates \"'Alim al-ghayb wa al-shahadah,\" knowledge that encompasses the absent and present, the secret talk of three where He is fourth and the open proclamation in gatherings where room is made or withheld. \"Seen\" includes the house destroyed with hands visible and the charity given openly; \"unseen\" includes what is hidden in the heart, the light prepared ahead and to the right, and the wall with a gate whose one side is grace and other torment. This knowledge underwrites the well-preserved Record where every calamity or blessing was written before creation and supports the promise that on the Day He will inform them of what they did.\n\n\"He is the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful\" closes with \"al-Rahman al-Rahim,\" the same pair that opened glorification as knower of mercy. After chapters that warned of severe, humiliating punishment, these names remind that divine knowledge is exercised with encompassing mercy. Compassion reaches all creation with provision, rain, fire, and guidance to bring from darkness to light. Mercy specifically embraces those who humble hearts, revive them, and lend good loans, granting double share, light, and forgiveness.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think mercy cancels the severe punishment warned about. The Quran holds both, showing mercy as the invitation and severe punishment as the outcome for persistent, knowing defiance that insists on denial until death.\n\nRemembering this verse in worship and decision helps balance awe and hope. The Knower sees private deeds, so sincerity matters more than show, and the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful is the One to whom every soul should look for what it has sent forth for tomorrow, trusting that humbled reflection will be met with gracious response.",
  "23": "\"He is Allah—there is no god except Him: the King, the Most Holy, the All-Perfect, the Source of Serenity, the Watcher of all, the Almighty, the Supreme in Might, the Majestic. Glorified is Allah far above what they associate with Him in worship!\" continues the litany of beautiful names, expanding from knowledge and mercy to sovereignty and perfection. The opening again stresses \"no god except Him,\" anchoring after calls not to forget Allah and not to equate residents of Fire and Paradise.\n\nEach title carries weight. \"The King\" translates \"al-Malik,\" absolute owner whose kingdom of heavens and earth is not borrowed or contested, unlike the temporary claims of Banu an-Nadir's strongholds. \"The Most Holy\" translates \"al-Quddus,\" free from any defect, deficiency, or impurity that touches creation. \"The All-Perfect\" translates \"al-Salam,\" the source of peace, wholeness, and safety, free from flaw and the giver of soundness to hearts that remember. \"The Source of Serenity\" is another facet of \"al-Mu'min,\" the granter of security and the confirmer of truth, who secures believers and confirms His messengers with clear proofs. \"The Watcher\" translates \"al-Muhaymin,\" the guardian and observer who oversees everything, knowing whatever goes into earth and ascends, the One who kept account while they forgot.\n\n\"The Almighty, the Supreme in Might, the Majestic\" translates \"al-'Aziz, al-Jabbar, al-Mutakabbir,\" a triad of irresistible power, compulsive authority that mends and compels, and greatness that is truly majestic. Al-Jabbar also carries the tender sense of mending the broken, relevant to a chapter that mended community through fair distribution and prayer without bitterness.\n\n\"Glorified is Allah far above what they associate with Him in worship\" closes with \"subhana Allah 'amma yushrikun,\" declaring flawlessness beyond partners. After exposing alliances with displeased peoples and oath-shields that associate others' favor with Allah's, this glorification cleanses monotheism.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to recite these names as mere poetry without trust. Knowing Him as King, Holy, Peace, Watcher, and Mighty should affect private talk, oaths, and room-making, as one lives under the Watcher who is also the Source of Peace, inviting reliance rather than grieving whispers.",
  "24": "\"He is Allah: the Creator, the Inventor, the Shaper. He alone has the Most Beautiful Names. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth constantly glorifies Him. And He is the Almighty, All-Wise.\" seals Surah al-Hashr by focusing on origination, beauty, and universal praise. The closing mirrors the opening \"Whatever in heavens and earth glorifies Allah, for He is the Almighty, All-Wise,\" forming a perfect frame.\n\n\"The Creator, the Inventor, the Shaper\" translates \"al-Khaliq, al-Bari', al-Musawwir,\" three stages of bringing into being. Creator is the One who measures and decrees creation. Inventor is the One who brings it into existence without prior model, free from defect, unlike human copies that need prototypes. Shaper is the One who fashions each thing with its distinct form, from the mountain that would be torn apart in awe to the seed that becomes a plant that delights then withers to chaff, to the human shaped from a drop and destined to be gathered for the appointed Day. Together they answer those who doubted resurrection after dust and bones, showing the same fashioning power that first shaped can reshape in forms unknown.\n\n\"He alone has the Most Beautiful Names\" affirms that no beautiful quality belongs to another at its source. Beautiful names include the King, the Holy, the Peace, and the earlier Most Compassionate, Most Merciful, each beautiful and perfect in meaning, unlike human titles that exaggerate or fade.\n\n\"Whatever is in the heavens and the earth constantly glorifies Him\" returns to continuous praise, now as present ongoing act. Heavens praised earlier in past tense, now glorify continuously, linking the mountain's imagined humility to the constant reality of cosmic worship that humans are invited to join.\n\n\"And He is the Almighty, All-Wise\" reaffirms the pair that makes mighty action wise. From expulsion without horses to gains distributed so wealth does not circulate only among the rich, might was guided by wisdom.\n\nA misunderstanding would be to think \"Shaper\" concerns only physical form. Shaping includes provision of law, balance, and purposeful tests like iron with benefits to prove who stands for Him. Remembering these three names fosters gratitude when shaped in ease and patience when shaped in hardship, confident that the Shaper who glorifies the heavens through praise will glorify repentant hearts through revival."
};